To create a C++ function that converts Markdown formatted text to HTML, you can use a third library like `markdowncpp`. Here's a simple example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <markdowncpp.h>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    markdowncpp::Markdown md;
    std::string html = md.convert(markdown);
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello, World!\nThis is a sample paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << "\n";
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses the `markdowncpp` library to convert the Markdown text to HTML. You can install the `markdowncpp` library using a package manager or CMake.

